window.DDIA = window.DDIA || {chapters:[], viz:[], system:null};
window.DDIA.chapters.push({
  n: 11, title: "Batch Processing",
  worksheet: "https://docs.google.com/document/d/12C76S4-MeUxf_eT3kAlWiA1IJxfeSebH0KAJjr9nvKs/edit",
  why: "Chapters 2 through 10 were about systems that answer a request while someone waits. This chapter is the other half of every real product: the jobs that run over everything you've stored, with nobody waiting, and produce something new from it: a search index, a model, a report, a backfill, a deletion proof. The interview trap is treating batch as a scheduling problem; the chapter's point is that it's a functional-programming problem. Immutable input, deterministic function, output you can throw away and rebuild. Every answer in this chapter comes back to that sentence. The round below is the messaging system's batch layer: the jobs behind its search index, inbox, analytics, abuse model and deletion guarantee. Two visualizations: the <a href=\"#/viz/batch-joins\">Batch Joins Stepper</a> and <a href=\"#/viz/etl-vs-elt\">ETL vs ELT</a>.",
  jargon: [
    ["Batch job", "Reads a fixed, complete input, runs for minutes to days, writes a complete output. Measured by throughput, not response time. Nobody waits on any one record."],
    ["Online / batch / stream", "Request-response, judged by response time; bounded input, judged by throughput; unbounded input, judged by the delay between an event and its effect. Three systems, three metrics."],
    ["Unix philosophy", "Small single-purpose programs, a uniform interface (a stream of lines), input never modified, logic separate from wiring. <code>sort | uniq -c</code> is MapReduce on one machine: sort is the shuffle, uniq -c is the reducer."],
    ["Immutable input, derived output", "The job only reads its input and writes to a new location. A bug means fix the code, discard the output, re-run. Nothing was destroyed. The property the whole chapter rests on."],
    ["Map", "One input record in, zero or more key-value pairs out. Filter, project, explode. Really flatMap; it can drop records, it just can't see two at once."],
    ["Shuffle", "Partition every emitted record to the reducer that owns its key (hash mod R), move it over the network, then sort by key so one key's values are adjacent. Where the network cost and the skew both live."],
    ["Reduce", "Walk one partition's sorted input once; all values for a key arrive together. Emits one output file per reducer; nothing merges across reducers afterward."],
    ["Combiner", "The reduce function applied on the mapper before the shuffle. Legal only for associative, commutative aggregations. Turns a hot key's million (user, 1) records into one per mapper."],
    ["Coordinator", "Hands out map and reduce tasks, watches completion, re-runs failures. Processes no data. A scheduler, not a chapter 10 leader: nothing for two of them to disagree about."],
    ["Data locality", "Input is cut into blocks (128 MB), each stored on three machines; the scheduler runs each mapper on a machine holding its block. The Hadoop-era picture; object storage weakened it."],
    ["Sort-merge join", "Both inputs through mappers emitting the join key, tagged by source; shuffle brings a key's records from both sides to one reducer; sort puts the small side's record first. Any size; everything crosses the network."],
    ["Broadcast hash join", "One side fits in memory. Copy it whole to every mapper, build a hash table, join as you read the big side. No shuffle. Breaks when the small side grows, usually silently over two years."],
    ["Partitioned hash join", "Both inputs already partitioned by the same key, hash and count. Mapper k loads only partition k of the small side. The broadcast join one partition at a time; the shuffle happened in an earlier job."],
    ["Hot key / skew", "One key with far more records than the rest lands on one reducer; the job finishes when that reducer does. No hash function can split one key. Fixes: combiner, salt plus replicated small side, map-side join for the hot keys, or a product cap."],
    ["Salt", "Append a suffix derived from the record (never a random draw) to a hot key so it spreads across N reducers; replicate the other side's record N ways for a join; sum N partials in a second stage for an aggregation."],
    ["Materialization", "Writing an intermediate result to durable replicated storage between stages. MapReduce did it after every job: restartable and debuggable, slow and no pipelining."],
    ["Dataflow engine", "Spark, Flink, Tez. The whole workflow is one graph of operators; output streams to the next operator in memory or local disk; recovery is recomputing a lost partition from its inputs. Same primitives, no forced sort, no do-nothing mappers."],
    ["Deterministic operator", "Same input, same output, every run. Required because a lost partition is recomputed after downstream tasks may already have read the first version; nondeterminism gives an output that silently mixes two versions."],
    ["Checkpoint", "Deliberately write one stage's output durably inside a dataflow job, to cap how far back a failure recomputes. MapReduce's behavior bought back for one stage."],
    ["Object storage + columnar files + table format", "Where batch data lives now: S3-style storage separate from compute, Parquet files (one column at a time, block statistics), and Iceberg/Delta/Hudi metadata giving the files a schema, snapshots, atomic commits and time travel. The lakehouse."],
    ["Warehouse vs lake", "A managed columnar system that owns its storage and enforces schema at load (Snowflake, BigQuery) vs open files you own with schema decided at read. The lakehouse blurs it. Not Postgres sharded."],
    ["ETL vs ELT", "Transform in code before loading, or load raw and transform with SQL in the warehouse. ELT keeps the raw copy (re-derive anything later); ETL moves less and masks before landing."],
    ["Bulk-loading the output", "A batch job writes the serving store's own files (index segments, sorted tables), the serving nodes copy them, a pointer flips atomically, the old set stays for rollback. Never one row at a time into a live database."],
    ["Derived data", "Anything regenerable from something else: an index, a cache, a view, a model, a feature table. The source is the truth; the derivation is a job; a wrong derivation is a code fix and a re-run."],
    ["Pregel / bulk synchronous parallel", "Think like a vertex: partition the graph once; in each superstep a vertex reads its messages, updates, sends to neighbors; a barrier between supersteps; stop when nobody sends. The graph never moves after partitioning; only messages do."],
    ["Stale vs incorrect", "Batch output is stale (behind, but right for its cutoff) and heals at the next run. An incrementally patched view is current and can be wrong forever: it has no memory of being correct. Only a rebuild from the source does."],
    ["Version by log position", "Every derived row carries the offset of the last event applied to it; a write lands only if its version is newer. Makes batch backfills and stream patches commutative. Last-writer-wins with a log position as the clock, which is the safe kind."],
    ["Crypto-shredding", "A per-user content key; destroying the key makes every copy of that user's data unreadable, including backups and lake files you cannot rewrite in time."]
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: "round", title: "The round: batch processing in the messaging system", intro: "The design is on the board: owners per conversation, Cassandra for messages, Postgres for conversations and inboxes, an event log. The interviewer turns to everything that runs over it with nobody waiting.", items: [
      { q: "\"What batch jobs does this system actually need? Input, output, cadence, who reads it.\"",
        a: "<p>The frame: batch output is anything the product <i>serves</i> that is too expensive to compute per request, plus anything that must be proven to have run. Analytics is the smallest category.</p><div class=\"tbl\"><table><tr><th>Job</th><th>Input</th><th>Output</th><th>Cadence</th><th>Reader</th></tr><tr><td>Search index rebuild</td><td>messages snapshot, participants</td><td>(user, term) segments, swapped in</td><td>nightly</td><td>search service</td></tr><tr><td>Inbox backfill + reconcile</td><td>messages, participants</td><td>per-user inbox rows, diff vs live</td><td>weekly, and on schema change</td><td>inbox read path; on-call if diff ≠ 0</td></tr><tr><td>Deletion + retention</td><td>deletion requests, all stores</td><td>tombstones per store, signed report</td><td>daily</td><td>regulator; compaction</td></tr><tr><td>Abuse model + risk scores</td><td>messages, reports, blocks, account age</td><td>model file, per-user score table</td><td>daily</td><td>send-path classifier, trust &amp; safety</td></tr><tr><td>Product metrics</td><td>event log</td><td>DAU, msgs/day, delivery p99, group-size distribution</td><td>hourly ops, daily product</td><td>dashboards, on-call, leadership</td></tr><tr><td>Cold tiering</td><td>messages older than a year</td><td>files in object storage + pointers</td><td>monthly</td><td>old-history reads; the storage bill</td></tr></table></div><p>Also: a hot-key sampler, per-user data export, people-you-may-know, storage quotas.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"The inbox is stream-maintained. Why is it on a batch list?\"", a: "<p>The live inbox is patched by the stream; the <i>backfill</i> and the <i>reconcile</i> are batch. The rule across the system: online serves, stream patches, batch rebuilds and checks.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "batch output is what the product serves but can't compute per request; the inbox's backfill and reconcile are batch even though its freshness isn't." },
      { q: "\"Walk me through the search index full rebuild: input, join, partitioning, and replacing it without downtime.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Tokenize:</b> one message in, one <code>((user_id, term), msg_id, position)</code> per word out; position enables phrase search. <b>The join:</b> \"all messages a user sent or received\" means fanning each message out to every participant, a join of messages with participants on <code>conv_id</code> before tokenizing; this is where the hot key lives. <b>Partition</b> by <code>hash(user_id)</code>, the inbox's hash, so a user's search and inbox share a shard; sort by (user_id, term); each reducer writes an immutable sorted index file, an LSM segment. <b>Replace:</b> new segment set to a new directory, serving nodes copy, a manifest pointer flips, the old set stays for rollback.</p><p>Incremental on top is not free: new small segments, every query consults all of them, periodic merges, deletes as tombstones. The nightly rebuild bounds the segment count and reconciles the incremental path. Both, not either.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"A 200,000-member group would be 200,000 postings per term per message. What do you do with the big groups?\"", a: "<p>Two indexes with different keys. Small conversations: <code>(user_id, term)</code>, fanned out per participant. Big ones: <code>(conv_id, term)</code>, indexed once, no fan-out; the query merges the user's own postings with each big conversation they belong to (from the participants table). Chapter 7's local-versus-global secondary index, chosen per conversation.</p>" },
          { q: "\"How does the indexer know which mode a conversation is in?\"", a: "<p>A stored flag on the conversation row (<code>index_mode</code>), not a live count. A job sets it with hysteresis (above 600 per-conversation, back below 400). Indexer and query path both read the flag so they cannot disagree; after a flip, read both indexes until the next rebuild has moved the history. A durable decision everyone reads, same shape as the owner epoch.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "explode to (user, term) after joining participants, shard by the inbox's hash, write segments, flip a manifest; incremental on top, rebuild underneath." },
      { q: "\"That 200,000-member group is the hot key in every per-conversation job. Show me the skew and fix it.\"",
        a: "<p>Inbox fan-out join, yesterday: the median group has 4 participants and 20 messages, so its reducer emits 80 rows; the big group has 200,000 participants and 1,000 messages, so its reducer emits <b>200,000,000</b> rows while its neighbors idle. Job time equals that reducer's time.</p><p><b>Fixes, in order:</b> do not fan out the big group at all (flag it to per-conversation storage, which removes the hot key instead of handling it); for jobs that must touch it, salt plus replicated small side, or a map-side join for the flagged conversations; cap group size as a product decision, because the biggest group sets the worst case of every per-conversation job. The trap: the online path and the batch job must read the same flag or data goes missing silently.</p>",
        line: "the biggest group is the worst case of every per-conversation job; cap it, and move anything above a few hundred to pull-based storage." },
      { q: "\"The index job takes 9 hours and the window is 6. Three levers, in order.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Step zero: read the stage timings.</b> One task running for hours while the rest finish in minutes is skew; all tasks uniformly slow is volume. The picture picks the first lever.</p><ol><li><b>Kill the skew:</b> the big group in the participants join; flag it, salt it, or map-side join it. Nine hours where eight were one reducer becomes ninety minutes.</li><li><b>Shrink the shuffle:</b> tokenize and project before the shuffle so <code>(user_id, term, msg_id, pos)</code> crosses, not the message row; drop deleted and media-only messages before the join; broadcast participants if it fits; read columnar, date-partitioned input.</li><li><b>Do less per night:</b> index only what changed into new segments and rebuild weekly, or roll the rebuild one user-shard per night.</li><li><b>Last, more machines:</b> one config line, only works for the uniform picture, costs money every night, and hides what levers 1 and 2 would have removed.</li></ol>",
        line: "measure, skew, shuffle, incremental, hardware; say \"add machines\" first and the follow-up is \"what if it's skew.\"" },
      { q: "\"Why does the stream-maintained inbox drift? I'd have thought batch was the one that goes stale.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Batch goes stale. Stream goes incorrect.</b> Staleness heals at the next run; incorrectness never does. The inbox copy is never computed, it is the sum of every delta ever applied, and each of these leaves a permanent dent: a duplicated event (at-least-once delivery, a crash between write and offset commit, an unread count off by one forever); a dropped event; out-of-order (a delete arriving before the send it refers to); old code (a bug from March to June, fixed in June, leaves every row it touched wrong); a schema change (months one through six have nothing in the new column). Lag reads zero the whole time.</p><p>The batch rebuild ignores the stream and recomputes from the source of truth, correct by construction for its cutoff. Diff it against the live copy as of the same cutoff; every mismatch is a drift bug. If the diff is always zero you have earned trust and can rebuild less often.</p>",
        line: "an incrementally maintained view has no memory of being correct; only a rebuild from the source does." },
      { q: "\"A new inbox field ships Monday. The weekly batch runs Tuesday midnight; a message lands at 12:01. How do the batch and the stream not clobber each other?\"",
        a: "<p><b>The cutoff is a position in the log, not a time on the clock.</b> The batch records that it read everything up to offset X; the 12:01 message is X+1.</p><p><b>Option A, version every row by log position.</b> Batch rows carry <code>version = X</code>; the stream writes event N with <code>version = N</code>; one rule for both: apply only if my version ≥ the row's. Batch landing after the stream applied X+1 is skipped; the reverse order applies X+1 on top. Last-writer-wins with a log position as the clock, the safe kind.</p><p><b>Option B, build new, replay, flip.</b> Write the inbox as a new table from the snapshot at X, run a consumer on it from offset X until lag is near zero, flip readers. Needs log retention for the rebuild's duration and an idempotent consumer.</p><p>B for schema changes and reconciliation; A as the permanent write rule so batch and stream writes commute. The diff compares only rows with version ≤ X, so post-cutoff events never show as false mismatches.</p>",
        line: "anchor the batch to a log offset; version rows by offset and apply-if-newer, or build new, replay from the offset, then flip." },
      { q: "\"Backfill edited_at across two years of history. What must the job never do to the live Cassandra cluster?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Value:</b> edits are events with <code>ref_seq</code>, so <code>edited_at</code> is the max edit timestamp per (conv_id, ref_seq). <b>Delivery:</b> input from a snapshot or the lake copy, never a live scan; the reducer emits Cassandra's native on-disk files partitioned by the cluster's token ranges (a partitioned hash join in the wild); bulk-load with the streaming loader, bypassing commit log and memtable, throttled per node, one rack at a time, off-peak; then wait for compaction.</p><p><b>Never:</b> write through the client path; scan the live cluster for input; <b>overwrite a newer live value</b> (the backfilled cell carries the edit event's timestamp as its write timestamp, so per-cell last-writer-wins keeps today's real edit); load faster than compaction absorbs. And ask first whether you need the column at all: it is derivable on read.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Why not stand up an offline cluster and swap over?\"", a: "<p>Too big a hammer for one column: a full copy, a double write window, and a cutover risk, to add a value that bulk-loaded SSTables add in place. Cluster swap is the answer for a storage-format change, not a field.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "emit the store's own files from a snapshot, bulk-load them throttled, version the cell by the event's time; the best backfill is the one you skip." },
      { q: "\"Join messages in Cassandra with users in Postgres to train the abuse model. Which join, and how do both inputs get to the same place?\"",
        a: "<p>Training needs features for <i>every</i> sender, abusers and normal users alike, or there is nothing to learn the difference from: account age, messages per day, reply rate, report count, contact overlap. No filter to push down.</p><p><b>Get both out:</b> messages via Cassandra's nightly snapshot to date-partitioned columnar files; users via a nightly dump or, better, Postgres's change log into the lake. Both anchored to a cutoff. <b>Join:</b> users is a few gigabytes, so broadcast hash on <code>sender_id</code> with a size check; sort-merge if it outgrows memory; partitioned hash is impossible by construction (two systems, two hashes). <b>Output:</b> a per-user feature table and a model file; the model reaches the send path by file swap.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Couldn't you scan messages for bad actors first and only look up those users?\"", a: "<p>That is a real optimization, pushing the filter below the join, but it belongs to inference, not training. At inference the send path joins the suspect against the feature table. Doing it for training would scan Cassandra live and point-look-up production Postgres, and would learn nothing about normal users.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "land both in the lake first; broadcast the small side with a guard; the filter-first trick is for inference, not training." },
      { q: "\"Daily actives, messages per day, p99 group size. Source, format, and why not the online stores?\"",
        a: "<p>The source is an <b>event log</b>: message_sent, message_delivered, conversation_opened, with user, conversation, timestamp, region, landed in object storage as columnar files partitioned by date. Daily actives is yesterday's directory, count distinct user; weekly actives is seven directories; messages per day is a row count. p99 group size is a state question: participants snapshot, count per <code>conv_id</code>, 99th percentile.</p><p><b>Why not online:</b> load; smeared reads with no cutoff; wrong shape (Cassandra is keyed by conversation, so \"yesterday\" is a full-cluster scan, and a timestamp secondary index on the write-heaviest table is what chapter 7 said never to build); wrong engine (no distinct count or percentile across a cluster); no history. Output: a tiny aggregate table, one row per day per metric, which is what the dashboard reads.</p>",
        line: "events to date-partitioned columnar files, state from snapshots, aggregates to a small table; the online stores have the wrong shape and no history." },
      { q: "\"The job ran over the 2:00 am snapshot and 2:05 am messages are missing from the dashboard. Bug? What do you tell the product manager?\"",
        a: "<p>By design: the cutoff is the feature, and the output is exactly right as of 2:00 am. <b>The bug would be the opposite:</b> some 2:05 messages in and some out is a smeared cutoff. <b>Give the product manager a contract:</b> \"data as of 2:00 am, published by 6:00 am, daily,\" with the as-of timestamp on the dashboard; anything fresher than a day is stream processing with the batch as the nightly reconcile.</p><p>The real edge is fuzzier than 2:00: a Cassandra snapshot is not one instant across nodes, so a 1:59:58 write to two of three replicas may or may not be in it. Process only up to a watermark with a safety margin, or anchor the cutoff to a log position.</p>",
        line: "a clean cutoff is the feature; publish the as-of time as a contract, and anchor it to a log position so it's exact." },
      { q: "\"A deleted user's data must be gone within 30 days. Batch or online, and how do you prove it ran?\"",
        a: "<p>Finding a user's data is not a point lookup: their messages sit under (conv_id, seq) across every conversation; copies sit in other users' inboxes; postings live under every recipient's (user_id, term); the lake holds them in immutable date files and backups keep them 35 days. So: the request is recorded and the account flagged <b>online</b> (reads hide the user within a minute); a <b>daily batch</b> runs over the whole pending list, one pass per snapshot finding every row for every pending user, emitting bulk tombstones, dropping postings at the next rebuild, rewriting affected lake files. A hundred requests cost the same scan as one.</p><p><b>Crypto-shredding</b> for the copies you cannot rewrite: per-user content key; destroy the key and every copy (backups, lake files, the ones you missed) is unreadable. It is what makes 35-day backups compatible with a 30-day promise.</p><p><b>Proof:</b> an audit row per request (received, flagged, each store's completion time and tombstone count, key destroyed); a separate <b>verification job</b> that scans the next snapshot and asserts zero rows for every user deleted more than a day ago, producing a signed daily report. Keep the report, not the data.</p>",
        line: "flag online, remove by daily batch over the pending list, shred the key for what you can't rewrite, prove it with a separate verifier." },
      { q: "\"The inbox rebuild wrote garbage for 5% of users. Recovery, and what property makes it possible?\"",
        a: "<p>Throw away the output, fix, re-run, verify, publish; or point back at yesterday's version. <b>Two properties:</b> immutable input lets you re-run, and the job wrote a new version instead of overwriting, so yesterday still exists. The inbox is also stream-fed, so rollback has a second step: re-point to yesterday's output and replay the stream from yesterday's offset, or the 95% lose today's messages.</p><p>And move verification <i>before</i> the flip: row count within a few percent of yesterday, a sampled diff against the live copy, schema checks; a failed gate leaves the pointer alone and pages on-call instead of shipping garbage.</p>",
        line: "re-run or re-point, then replay the stream from the old offset; and gate the flip so the garbage never goes live." }
    ]},
    { id: "mechanisms", title: "Mechanisms, probed on the design", intro: "The chapter's machinery, asked the way an interviewer asks it: what it is, when you choose it, where it breaks.", items: [
      { q: "\"Batch versus online versus stream. One sentence each, and the metric for each.\"",
        a: "<p><b>Online:</b> a client sends a request and waits; the metric is response time (p99). <b>Batch:</b> a bounded, complete input to a complete output, nobody waiting on any one record; the metric is throughput, or how long the whole job takes. <b>Stream:</b> an unbounded input, output shortly after each event; the metric is the delay between event and effect, plus lag when the consumer falls behind.</p><p>Batch's real advantage, stated sharp: the input is immutable and the output derived, so a bug means fix the code and re-run. Nothing is destroyed.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"When do you precompute in batch instead of doing the work at request time?\"", a: "<p>When the work is too expensive while someone waits (a scan or join over everything), when the answer can be computed ahead for every key and served as a lookup (recommendations, a search index, per-user aggregates), and when you want the result re-runnable. Precompute in batch, serve online.</p>" },
          { q: "\"And what does that cost you?\"", a: "<p>Staleness, as old as the last run; cold start, a new user has no row until the next run so you need an online fallback; wasted work, results for every key most never asked for; the window, a job longer than its period never catches up; late failure, a job that dies in hour eight cost eight hours.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "three systems, three metrics: response time, throughput, event-to-effect delay." },
      { q: "\"Why does the chapter open with Unix pipes? What property of sort | uniq -c is MapReduce copying?\"",
        a: "<p>The pipe design is what the chapter generalizes: a uniform interface (lines; for MapReduce, key-value files), input never modified, logic separated from wiring so stages compose, re-order, and debug one at a time, and need no coordination because they only talk through files.</p><p>The specific property: <b>sorting puts identical keys adjacent, so one sequential pass with one counter finishes each key.</b> That is the shuffle. <code>sort</code> is the shuffle and <code>uniq -c</code> is the reducer. The lesson is composable stages over immutable input, not a speed argument about memory.</p>",
        line: "sort is the shuffle, uniq -c is the reducer; the lesson is composable stages over immutable input, not shell vs Python." },
      { q: "\"Walk one MapReduce job end to end: count messages per user. Where is the network cost?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Input:</b> the log in 128 MB blocks, each on three machines, ordered by time. <b>Map:</b> runs where the block is (no network), emits <code>(user_id, 1)</code> per record. <b>Shuffle:</b> partition each record to reducer <code>hash(user_id) mod R</code>, then sort within the reducer. <b>Reduce:</b> one pass, sum per user, one output file per reducer.</p><p><b>The network cost is the shuffle:</b> one record per message crosses. A <b>combiner</b> pre-sums on each mapper so a heavy user arrives as one record per mapper instead of one per message. Output replication is a second, smaller cost. The hash decides the destination; it does not reduce traffic.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Who's in charge, and who does the merging?\"", a: "<p>A 1 TB log is ~8,000 blocks, each on three of a thousand nodes, so each node holds ~3/1000 of the data. A <b>coordinator</b> hands out tasks and retries failures; it processes no data and needs no consensus because it holds nothing two coordinators could disagree about. Each of R reducers pulls its slice from every mapper, merges the already-sorted pieces, and reduces; nothing merges across reducers. The output is R files.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "mappers read locally and emit pairs, the shuffle partitions and sorts, reducers aggregate once; the cost is the shuffle and a combiner shrinks it." },
      { q: "\"Three joins: sort-merge, broadcast hash, partitioned hash. When each, and what breaks each?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Sort-merge:</b> both sides through mappers emitting the join key tagged by source; the shuffle brings a key's records from both sides to one reducer; sort puts the small side first. Works at any size, the safe default. Breaks: both datasets cross the network, the most expensive join in traffic; a hot key stalls one reducer; you pay to sort both sides; wasted when inputs are already co-partitioned.</p><p><b>Broadcast hash:</b> the small side fits in memory on every mapper, build a hash table, join as you read the big side; no shuffle, and a mapper's work is capped by its block so no skew. Breaks: the small side stops fitting, silently, after two years of growth, and yesterday's job dies of out-of-memory today with no code change (needs a size check and a fallback); copy cost is small-side × mappers; optimizer thresholds flip plans night to night when the table hovers near the limit.</p><p><b>Partitioned hash:</b> both sides large and already partitioned the same way (same key, hash, count); mapper k loads only partition k of the small side; no shuffle. Breaks: the partitioning must be identical and independently produced datasets almost never match; it serves one key (bucketed by user helps no join on conversation); done just for one join it saves nothing, because producing co-partitioned inputs <i>is</i> a shuffle.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Rule of thumb?\"", a: "<p>Both big, sort-merge and pay the shuffle. One side fits in memory, broadcast and pay small-side × mappers, with a size guard. Both already laid out by an earlier job, partitioned hash: chapter 7's co-located shards.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "both sides big → sort-merge and pay the shuffle; one side fits in memory → broadcast and pay users × mappers; already co-partitioned → partitioned hash, because someone already paid." },
      { q: "\"A hot key. What does it do to the runtime, and how do you handle it?\"",
        a: "<p>All of one key's records land on one reducer; a thousand-machine job runs at the speed of one. No hash function can split one key, and a bigger machine for it does not scale. <b>Fixes:</b> a combiner, which alone removes the skew for a plain count; salt the key with a suffix derived from the record (two stages for an aggregation; replicate the other side N ways for a join); broadcast the hot keys' small-side records and map-side join them, sort-merge the rest. Finding the hot keys first: a sampling pass or table metadata.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Does a map-side join for the hot keys still pile the hot key on one machine?\"", a: "<p>No, and not because of randomization: <b>mappers are assigned by input block, reducers by key.</b> The hot user's messages are scattered through the log by time, so each mapper sees only the ones in its block and joins them there. Skew only appears when you group by key, and a map-side join never groups. A mapper's work is capped by block size; a reducer's by nothing.</p>" },
          { q: "\"What does salting cost?\"", a: "<p>A hot-key list first, which goes stale; a salt that must be deterministic or a retried mapper double-counts and drops records; N copies of the small-side record per hot key; a second stage for aggregations; two code paths if you go hybrid. And it spreads over reducers you already have, it does not need more nodes.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "no hash splits one key; salt it, map-side join it, or pre-aggregate it, and find it first with a sampling pass." },
      { q: "\"Why did dataflow engines replace MapReduce, and what did they keep?\"",
        a: "<p><b>MapReduce wasted:</b> every stage's output written to the distributed filesystem three ways to be read once; no stage starts until the previous finishes entirely; a forced sort on every shuffle; mappers that only re-read the previous reducer's output; a join plus a group plus a sort is three jobs with do-nothing mappers, hence SQL layers on top. A whole-graph view also lets an optimizer reorder joins and push filters down.</p><p><b>Kept:</b> the primitives (partition by key, shuffle, sort-merge and hash joins, group and aggregate), the functional contract (immutable input, no side effects), fault tolerance by re-execution, and the storage layer for inputs and final outputs. What is skipped is the durable replicated write, not disks: fused operators pass records as function calls, shuffle boundaries buffer in memory and spill to local, unreplicated disk. Recovery changed shape: re-read a materialized file became recompute from inputs.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"When would you still materialize between stages?\"", a: "<p>When stages fail often, when you are debugging or building one stage, and when several downstream jobs read the same intermediate dataset, so it is not really intermediate. In a dataflow job that is a <b>checkpoint</b> at an expensive or widely shared stage, to cap how far back a late failure recomputes.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "same primitives and the same contract; dropped the forced disk writes, the forced sort, and the forced waiting; checkpoint the expensive stages." },
      { q: "\"Why must a dataflow operator be deterministic, and what is the failure when it isn't?\"",
        a: "<p>A node dies <i>after</i> some downstream tasks already consumed its output. The engine recomputes the lost partition; if the operator is nondeterministic the recomputed partition differs, and tasks that read it after the failure see a different version from the ones that read it before. The final output <b>mixes two versions of the same data</b>: records counted twice, records missing, join rows that match nothing, and the job reports success.</p><p>Concrete: salt a hot key with a random number, re-run a mapper after a failure, and some of the hot user's messages go to two reducers and others to none. Derive the salt from the record instead. Usual sources: random without a seed, the clock, hash-table iteration order, an external lookup that changed. Remedies: make it deterministic, recompute everything downstream too, or checkpoint that stage. Materialization is the <i>cure</i> for nondeterminism, since every retry reads the same written file.</p>",
        line: "nondeterminism plus recompute-on-failure gives an output that silently mixes two versions; the job still says success." },
      { q: "\"Why never write batch output row by row into a live database? What instead?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Three reasons:</b> load (a thousand tasks at batch speed swamp a database sized for users); throughput (one round trip per record is orders of magnitude slower than a sequential file); and <b>it breaks all-or-nothing</b>: a failed task's rows are already visible, the retry writes them again, and frameworks run duplicate copies of slow tasks on purpose.</p><p><b>Instead:</b> the job writes the serving store's own files (index segments, sorted immutable tables) to the distributed filesystem under the same all-or-nothing guarantee; serving nodes copy them while still answering from the old set; a pointer (symlink, manifest, catalog row) flips atomically; the old set stays for rollback. Same trick as table-format commits and LSM segments. Softer alternative: write to a log and let the store consume at its own rate with idempotent writes (chapter 12).</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"What breaks with the pointer flip?\"", a: "<p>Disk: two full copies coexist at the swap and old versions pile up until pruned. The flip is atomic but the warm-up is not: cold caches spike latency for minutes, so pre-warm or shift traffic gradually. A one-record change means a full rebuild. The pointer itself needs care: two publishers racing on a shared file can point at a half-copied directory, so put it in a coordination service or a catalog with compare-and-set.</p>" },
          { q: "\"What does immutable input, throwaway output buy you when the job has a bug?\"", a: "<p>Re-run as many times as it takes; the bug cost compute and nothing else. The framework can re-run failed tasks automatically because tasks have no side effects. Rollback is pointing back at the previous output, which still exists because the new run wrote to a new location. Risky changes ship because a mistake is cheap and reversible. The condition: no side effects outside the output files.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "build the store's files inside the job, copy, flip a pointer, keep the old set; never one row at a time." },
      { q: "\"Where does batch data live now? What changed since the Hadoop picture?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Where:</b> object storage with storage separated from compute; a cluster is rented for the job and discarded, and data locality matters less. <b>Format:</b> columnar files (Parquet), one column at a time, compressed, block statistics for skipping. <b>On top:</b> table formats (Iceberg, Delta, Hudi) that give a pile of files a schema, snapshots, atomic commits and time travel; the atomic commit is the file swap generalized. <b>Interface:</b> SQL and dataframes with an optimizer; nobody hand-writes map and reduce, and warehouses and batch engines converged on the same storage. The driver was cost and elasticity. What is loose in a lake is <i>when</i> the schema is enforced (read, not load), not whether the data has shape.</p>",
        followups: [
          { q: "\"Lakehouse or warehouse, and where does the lakehouse hurt?\"", a: "<p>Lakehouse when many engines need the same data (Spark for the index, a model trainer, a deletion job, SQL for analysts) and cost at petabyte scale dominates. It hurts on small files (per-request pricing, slow listing, compaction becomes your job), no in-place update (an object is replaced whole; the table format fakes updates with new files plus a commit), no point lookups (an analytics store, never a serving store), concurrent writers needing a catalog that serializes commits, and the swamp: nothing forces schema or ownership. Warehouses sell governance for a reason; most end up with both.</p>" },
          { q: "\"ELT or ETL?\"", a: "<p>ELT (load raw, transform in SQL) when you want to keep the raw copy to re-derive anything later, storage is cheap, transforms as SQL are versioned and owned by analysts, and many consumers derive views from one raw copy. It breaks when sensitive data must be masked before landing (ETL by definition), when the transform is not SQL (parsing binary, running a model), when compute cost moves to the metered warehouse, and when you need a sliver of a huge source: load the sliver. See <a href=\"#/viz/etl-vs-elt\">ETL vs ELT</a>.</p>" }
        ],
        line: "object storage, Parquet, a table format on top, SQL in front; schema-on-read, not shapeless blobs." },
      { q: "\"Rebuild derived data from scratch, or patch it incrementally?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Rebuild</b> when the full job fits the window or correctness matters more than freshness: correct by construction, nothing drifts, a bug fix is a re-run; the cost scales with the whole dataset and the output is a day stale. <b>Patch</b> when the change rate is tiny relative to the total and the output must be fresh: the inbox copy, an unread counter, a cache; the cost is drift, since a mis-handled event leaves a wrong state forever, deletes and out-of-order updates are hard, and the state's correctness depends on every version of code that ever touched it. In practice both: patch for freshness, rebuild on a schedule to reconcile so drift has a bounded lifetime.</p><p>Rollback differs: a rebuild keeps the previous output and re-points; a patched view keeps the input log and a checkpoint and replays with fixed code. Nondeterminism is not the deciding factor.</p>",
        line: "a patched view has no memory of being correct; patch for now, rebuild for right, diff to find out how far apart." },
      { q: "\"Graph processing. When Pregel, when a join per hop?\"",
        a: "<p>Pregel (bulk synchronous parallel) when the iteration count is large or unknown (PageRank, shortest paths, connected components) and the graph needs a cluster. Partition vertices once; supersteps separated by a <b>barrier</b>: read messages, update, send to neighbors; stop when nobody sends. The graph never moves, only messages do; late rounds touch only vertices still changing; fault tolerance by periodic checkpoint of all vertex state. A join per hop reshuffles the entire edge table every iteration.</p><p>Where it breaks: the graph must fit in cluster memory; partitioning decides everything and real graphs partition badly (a social graph has no clean cut, so most messages cross machines anyway); the barrier waits for the slowest machine, so a celebrity vertex with fifty million edges sets the superstep's time; on small graphs a single-machine algorithm wins.</p>",
        line: "one or two hops, join; many rounds on a big graph, Pregel, and the barrier makes skew the cost again." },
      { q: "\"Your job needs the users table. Read production directly, take a snapshot, or consume its change log?\"",
        a: "<p><b>Direct:</b> a small table on a quiet night, and nothing else. A two-hour scan of a live database is not fresh, it is smeared: rows from minute 5 and minute 115 come from different states, a transaction can be counted in both states or neither, and it couples the job to the live schema; a read replica fixes load but still smears. <b>Snapshot:</b> a consistent point in time, no production load, re-runnable, a schema contract; the simple reliable default, stale by the interval and a full copy every night. <b>Change log:</b> stream the replication log into the lake continuously: consistent order, near-zero staleness, replayable history, many consumers, no load on the primary beyond one more replica; the cost is a pipeline to operate, and reconstructing \"the table as of midnight\" from a stream takes real machinery (chapter 12).</p>",
        line: "direct only for a small table on a quiet night; snapshot for simple and consistent; change log when you need fresh or many readers." }
    ]},
    { id: "followups", title: "Interviewer follow-ups", intro: "The pushes that come after the batch layer is on the board.", items: [
      { q: "\"Why not just run the analytics query against the production database at 3 am?\"",
        a: "<p>No guarantee it finishes before load returns, so you kill it and got nothing for the cost; 3 am is somebody's peak if you are international; the result is wrong-shaped anyway (no cutoff, and the online store cannot do a distinct count or percentile across a cluster); it couples analytics to the production schema so a column rename breaks a dashboard; and it works for a year until the table grows and the job creeps into the European peak.</p>",
        line: "it's load you can't bound, a result you can't trust, a coupling you'll regret, and it works until it doesn't." },
      { q: "\"What's the difference between a data warehouse and a data lake, and which here?\"",
        a: "<p>Warehouse: a managed columnar analytics system that owns its storage, enforces schema at load, handles compaction, indexing and access control, SQL only (Snowflake, BigQuery, Redshift). Not a row store sharded. Lake: files you own in object storage, schema at read, any engine. Lakehouse: a lake with a table format giving it the warehouse's transactions and schema.</p><p><b>Here:</b> the data feeds things that are not SQL (the search index build, model training, the deletion and reconcile jobs), so the lake is the system of record for the event log and the snapshots, one copy read by every engine, with a warehouse-style SQL layer on top for analysts reading the same files.</p>",
        line: "the warehouse sells a managed governed SQL box; the lake sells open files any tool reads; here, lake as source, SQL as one consumer." },
      { q: "\"Your nightly job failed halfway. What state is the system in?\"",
        a: "<p><b>If designed right: exactly the state it was in before the job started.</b> Input untouched; half-finished files are scratch nobody reads; the pointer never moved so yesterday's version is still served; no side effects escaped. Recovery is fix or wait, then re-run, resuming from the last checkpoint. The only cost is a day more staleness, and missing the window should page.</p><p><b>If not:</b> you do not know, and that is the point: an unknown fraction of rows updated in place, an unknown subset of notifications sent and about to be re-sent, maybe production down with it.</p>",
        line: "same state as before it started, because output goes to a new location and the pointer flips only on success." },
      { q: "\"MapReduce is dead. Why are we still talking about it?\"",
        a: "<p>Spark and Flink reimplement its model rather than run on it. The primitives are unchanged (partition by key, shuffle, sort-merge and hash joins, skew handling); the cost model is unchanged (\"where's the shuffle\" is still how you reason about any batch job); the fault-tolerance contract is unchanged (immutable input, no side effects, output only on success); and it is the vocabulary: \"what does your mapper emit\" is a Spark question in MapReduce words. Dead the way assembly is dead.</p>",
        line: "nobody writes it and everything compiles down to its ideas." },
      { q: "\"How do you know the index the batch job built is correct before you swap it in?\"",
        a: "<p>Unchanged code on changed data is the most common way a nightly job goes wrong, so the gates are on the <i>output</i>, run inside the job before the pointer moves: (1) every task completed; (2) <b>volume against yesterday</b>: total postings, rows per shard, file sizes within a few percent, no shard at zero (the cheapest gate, catches the most); (3) format: segments parse, keys sorted, no duplicates; (4) distribution: postings per user, distinct terms, top hundred terms close to yesterday's shape (catches a tokenizer regression); (5) <b>sampled correctness</b>: a thousand random messages found under every participant, a thousand deleted ones absent, plus golden queries including a big-group member; (6) diff against the live incremental index for rows older than the cutoff; (7) canary then phased rollout by user shard, watching zero-result rate, latency, errors; (8) old version retained a full day.</p>",
        line: "the job doesn't publish until it has proven its output: volume, format, distribution, samples, diff, canary." },
      { q: "\"Explain a shuffle to a junior engineer in three sentences.\"",
        a: "<p>After the mappers emit key-value pairs, the shuffle hashes each key to pick which reducer owns it and sends every record over the network to that machine. Each reducer then sorts what it received, so all values for one key sit next to each other. That is why the reducer can finish each key in a single pass, and why the shuffle is where a job's network cost and its skew both come from.</p>",
        line: "hash to pick the owner, move it, sort it; one pass per key, and that's where the cost and the skew live." },
      { q: "\"The job is deterministic, but two runs produced different output. Name three causes.\"",
        a: "<p>Every cause is a hidden input. The input was not actually fixed (snapshot cutoff differed, read a live table, an upstream file replaced). The clock. Order the code did not control: hash-map iteration, \"first value wins\" over unsorted values, floating-point sums in partition order. An external lookup fetched at run time: a dictionary, a model, a config, a flag. The environment: a library upgrade, a tokenizer version, locale or timezone. Random without a seed. And the non-cause to rule out first: output files written in task-completion order; sort before you diff.</p>",
        line: "a job is deterministic only over all its inputs, and the hidden ones are the clock, iteration order, and anything fetched at run time." },
      { q: "\"How would you make this pipeline incremental instead of a full rebuild every night?\"",
        a: "<p>Consume the change log instead of a snapshot; the job never ends and reads from an offset. Each event becomes a small write: new index segments merged periodically with deletes as tombstones (the LSM engine), or one inbox row update. Every write carries the event's offset as its version with apply-if-newer. Checkpoint the offset with the state so a crash resumes and replays harmlessly. The cutoff becomes a watermark.</p><p><b>What stays batch:</b> the reconcile (the incremental state drifts), schema changes (a backfill), compaction and tombstone cleanup, anything needing a consistent cutoff like the deletion proof. <b>Lambda:</b> batch for correctness and stream for freshness, merged at query time; robust, twice the code. <b>Kappa:</b> stream only, rebuilds by replaying the log through the same consumer; one code path, demands full retention and fast replay.</p>",
        line: "consume the log, version by offset, checkpoint; keep the batch for reconcile, schema changes, and compaction." }
    ]}
  ],
  takeaways: [
    "A batch job is a pure function over immutable input to derived output in a new location. Everything else, re-runs, rollback, automatic retry, safe experimentation, falls out of that one rule, and it holds only if the job has no side effects.",
    "Mappers are assigned by input block and reducers by key. A mapper's work is capped by the block; a reducer's by nothing. Skew is born at the shuffle, and no hash function can split one key.",
    "The shuffle is partition then sort: hash the key to pick the owner, move it over the network, sort so one key's values are adjacent. It is where the network cost and the skew both live, and a combiner is how you shrink it for an aggregation.",
    "Three joins by one question: does a side fit in memory? Both big, sort-merge (everything crosses). One fits, broadcast (ship it, no shuffle, guard the size). Both already partitioned the same way, partitioned hash (no shuffle, but someone already paid for it).",
    "Hot keys: find them first (sample or declare), then salt from the record and replicate the other side, or map-side join them, or pre-aggregate, or cap them as a product decision. The biggest group sets the worst case of every per-conversation job.",
    "Dataflow engines kept MapReduce's primitives and contract and dropped the forced disk writes, sorts, and waiting. The price is recompute-on-failure, which demands deterministic operators and earns checkpoints at expensive stages.",
    "Never write batch output row by row into a live store. Build the store's own files, copy them, flip a pointer, keep the old set. It is the only way the output stays all-or-nothing and the only way rollback is one pointer.",
    "Batch goes stale; a patched view goes incorrect. Online serves it, the stream keeps it fresh, the batch rebuilds it from the source and diffs to find how far the stream drifted. Both, not either.",
    "Anchor every cutoff to a log position, not a clock, and version derived rows by that position with apply-if-newer. That is what lets a batch backfill and a live stream write the same rows without clobbering each other.",
    "Know which store you are talking to. Cassandra, a Parquet lake, and a warehouse each have a shape; the right answer for one (write a column file, bulk-load SSTables, swap a cluster) is wrong for the others, and the chapter is mostly about putting the data where the job can use it."
  ]
});
